  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1230 AM MDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nTuesday...Upper ridge axis shifts slightly to the east, which allows \na weak front to drop south across the eastern plains, with moist \neasterly flow pushing back into eastern CO. This shift also allows a \ngradual increase in moisture advection up across the Desert SW and \ninto western CO. Latest numerical guidance does not indicate any \ncooling, in fact high temps stay nearly the same as Monday forecast. \nHowever, this pattern shift will allow a slow increase in \nprecipitation chances across the higher terrain, with isolated \nshowers spilling over onto the I-25 Corridor through the late \nafternoon and evening. As precip chances go up, so does the concern \nfor flash flooding potential across area burn scars. Feel that both \nthe Willow and the Aspen Acres Fires will need to be monitored \nclosely by late afternoon into the evening. Though forecast QPF is \nnot impressive, one over-performing storm could create a \ndangerous situation. Plan on highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s\nfor the high valleys, and mid 90s to lower 100s for the plains.\n\nWednesday through Friday...Long range model trends indicate a rather \nstrong upper shortwave crossing the Rocky Mt region Wed into Thu, \nthen the upper ridge builds back overhead starting Fri. Looking at \nthe 1000-500 mb RH, there is an impressive slug of moisture \nstreaming up across the Four Corners and then across the Rockies \nthrough Thu, lending some confidence to the likely/categorical pops \nmodels are spitting out for essentially all of the higher terrain \ninto Fri morning. Temps will remain hot, but the chance for showers \nand thunderstorms significantly increase starting Wed and continue \ninto the weekend. The threat of flash flooding over area burn scars \nbecomes much more of a concern starting Wed. Currently, Fri is the \n\"coolest\" day, with highs in the 80s for the high valleys, and upper \n80s to upper 90s for the plains.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...As the ridge builds back overhead, it looks \nlike moisture trapped under the high will recirculate each day, \nfueling afternoon and evening activity. Temps climb back up to \naround 90F for the high valleys, and mid 90s to low 100s for the \nplains, with scattered to likely pops for all of the higher terrain \nand I-25 Corridor. Moore",
